   "bodyText": "<p paraeid=\"{0ce4d9f8-0e7c-4940-8307-17b26ebbaa8f}{132}\" paraid=\"1452691630\">This building was originally referred to as the Commandant’s Residence or Government House. The first government buildings were constructed primarily of lumber, but the U.S. Navy quickly learned that wood did not hold up well in American Samoa’s tropical climate. Termites, heavy rain, and hurricanes made concrete a much more practical building material. </p> <p paraeid=\"{0ce4d9f8-0e7c-4940-8307-17b26ebbaa8f}{152}\" paraid=\"1604456526\">In 1902, the Naval Commandant wrote that if he were to be appointed Governor of American Samoa, he would need a proper residence and establishment. Construction of this building began in 1902 and was completed in July 1903. </p> <p paraeid=\"{0ce4d9f8-0e7c-4940-8307-17b26ebbaa8f}{158}\" paraid=\"1733848104\">The building is two stories high, with an attic, and sits on concrete piers. Its floor plan is cruciform, or cross-shaped. </p> <p paraeid=\"{0ce4d9f8-0e7c-4940-8307-17b26ebbaa8f}{164}\" paraid=\"1178215163\">When it was first completed, the building was painted entirely white. In 1930, red and green trim was added, giving it a more colorful appearance. At a later point, the building was painted white again. </p>",
   "audioDescription": "",
